Around 201 million years ago, another mass extinction wiped out many of the dinosaurs' rivals. Dinosaurs came through and spread everywhere. During the Jurassic, giant long-necked [[sauropod|sauropods]] like [[@barapasaurus|Barapasaurus]] in India grew enormous, and Stegosaurus roamed North America.

The Cretaceous brought new kinds: horned dinosaurs, duck-billed dinosaurs, armoured ankylosaurs and the great tyrannosaurs. Flowering plants appeared and spread during this time. Many dinosaurs we know best, like T. rex and [[@triceratops-horns|Triceratops]], lived only in the last few million years of it.

{{story:earth-age}}

Without [[gravity|gravity]], the body stops working hard to hold itself up. Muscles shrink, and bones can lose roughly 1 per cent of their strength each month in places like the hips and spine. Fluid shifts up towards the head, making faces puffy and legs thin.

{{story:space-body}}

So astronauts [[@exercise|exercise]] for about two hours every day. They run on a treadmill held down by elastic straps, pedal an exercise bike with no seat, and lift "weights" on a machine that uses [[vacuum|vacuum]] cylinders to push back. Even so, after months in orbit, many need help walking when they first return to Earth.

Leopards eat deer, wild pigs, monkeys and porcupines, but around Mumbai a large share of their food is [[@dogs-domestic|stray dogs]]. Dogs gather wherever rubbish piles up, and the forest edge has plenty of both.

In 2018 a team of scientists estimated that the park's leopards were eating hundreds of stray dogs every year. That may mean fewer dog bites and fewer cases of rabies, a deadly disease spread by bites. Nobody planned it, but the leopards may be quietly helping with public health.

{{story:mumbai}}

Ear defenders use hard plastic cups that reflect sound away. Inside, soft foam absorbs what gets through. A cushion around the edge seals each cup against your head. Even a small gap, from hair or the arms of a pair of glasses, can let in a lot of noise.

Airport ground crew, drummers and people mowing big lawns wear them. Many children who find crowds or fireworks overwhelming use them too.

{{story:noise-cancelling}}

In July 1881, US President James Garfield was shot. Doctors could not find the bullet. Alexander Graham Bell, already famous for the [[@telephone-history|telephone]], rushed to build a machine that would hum in an earpiece when it passed over metal.

He tested it at the president's bedside, but the results were confusing. One likely reason was the metal springs in the mattress. Garfield died that September. Bell's idea, though, was sound, and it pointed the way to the detectors we use now.

{{story:archaeology}}

At first, PTFE was used in factories and scientific equipment, where its resistance to chemicals was useful. Then, in France in the early 1950s, an engineer named Marc Grégoire coated his fishing tackle with it to stop lines from tangling. His wife, Colette, suggested he try it on her cooking pans.

It worked. In 1956, Grégoire started a company called Tefal to sell non-stick pans. By the early 1960s, they had reached the USA. Today they are in kitchens everywhere, including Indian ones where non-stick tawas turn out [[@dosa-idli|dosas]] and rotis with very little oil.

{{story:inventions-accidents}}

In many Indian towns, city water arrives only for a few hours a day. So homes store it. Water flows into an underground tank called a sump. An electric motor then pumps it up to a plastic or concrete tank on the roof, often a black one you can see from the street.

From the roof tank, water flows down whenever you open a tap. This setup works even when the city supply stops or the power goes out, as long as the tank has water. The catch is pressure. A shower on the top floor, just a few metres below the tank, may only dribble.

{{story:heart-beat}}

कुचिपुड़ी का नाम आंध्र प्रदेश के कृष्णा ज़िले के एक गाँव से आया है। परंपरा कहती है कि सिद्धेंद्र योगी नाम के एक संत ने वहाँ *भामाकलापम* नाम का एक नृत्य-नाटक रचा, जो कृष्ण की पत्नी सत्यभामा के बारे में है, और उसे गाँव के नौजवानों को सिखाया। इतिहासकार ठीक-ठीक नहीं जानते कि वे कब हुए।

सदियों तक कुचिपुड़ी के कुछ ब्राह्मण परिवारों के पुरुषों की मंडलियाँ एक गाँव से दूसरे गाँव घूमतीं और लंबे-लंबे नृत्य-नाटक खेलतीं। स्त्रियों के किरदार भी पुरुष ही निभाते थे। गीत इलाके की भाषा तेलुगु में होते थे, और कलाकार नाचने के साथ-साथ कभी-कभी संवाद भी बोलते थे।

{{story:waves-how}}

Aryabhata tells us his own age. In his book, he says he was 23 years old when 3,600 years of the current age, the Kali Yuga, had passed. That works out to 499 CE, so he was born in 476 CE.

He wrote that he worked in Kusumapura, which most historians identify with [[@bihar-history|Pataliputra]], near modern Patna in Bihar. It was a great centre of learning during the [[@gupta-golden-age|Gupta age]]. Some scholars think he came from elsewhere, possibly the south, partly because his work was most closely studied in Kerala.

{{story:gupta-golden-age}}

Many plants grow new leaves or seeds at an angle of about 137.5 degrees from the last one. This is called the golden angle, because it splits a circle according to the golden ratio. It packs seeds tightly with no gaps and helps leaves avoid shading each other.

The result is the [[@maths-golden-spiral|spirals]] you can count on sunflowers, pine cones and pineapples. They usually come in neighbouring [[@fibonacci-nature|Fibonacci numbers]], such as 34 and 55. This is one place where the golden ratio really is built into nature.

{{story:fibonacci-nature}}

In 1984, the Japanese puzzle company Nikoli began printing the puzzle. It called it *Sūji wa dokushin ni kagiru*, which roughly means "the digits must be single". That was soon shortened to Sudoku.

The puzzle became popular in Japan, but stayed little known elsewhere. Then Wayne Gould, a retired judge from [[@new-zealand|New Zealand]] who had worked in Hong Kong, wrote a computer program to make Sudoku puzzles. In 2004 he persuaded *The Times* newspaper in London to print them. Within a year, Sudoku was in [[@newspapers|newspapers]] across Britain, the United States, India and dozens of other countries, often next to [[@crosswords|the crossword]].

{{story:board-games}}

[[@goan-food|Goan food]] mixes Konkan cooking with Portuguese influence. Fish curry with rice is the everyday meal for many families. Vindaloo comes from a Portuguese dish of meat in wine and garlic, which Goan cooks changed with vinegar and spices. Bebinca is a layered dessert made from coconut milk, eggs and sugar, baked layer by layer.

The Portuguese also brought plants from the Americas, among them [[@cashew|cashews]], pineapples and [[@chillies|chillies]]. Today Goa's cashew nuts are famous, and the cashew fruit is used to make a drink called feni.

{{story:chillies}}

When a volcano erupts under a glacier, the heat melts the ice very fast, and floods of meltwater and rock can burst out. In 2010, an eruption at Eyjafjallajökull threw up a cloud of ash that drifted over Europe. For days, many thousands of flights were cancelled because ash can damage [[@plane-jet|jet engines]].

New land is still being made. In 1963, an eruption under the sea built a new island, Surtsey, off Iceland's south coast. Scientists have watched since then as plants, insects and birds arrived on the bare rock. Since 2021, a string of eruptions on the Reykjanes [[peninsula|peninsula]] near Reykjavík has sent [[lava|lava]] flowing towards roads and a fishing town.

{{story:old-faithful}}

The ice sheet has built up over hundreds of thousands of years, one snowfall at a time. Each year's snow gets pressed into a thin layer of ice, with tiny air bubbles trapped inside. Scientists drill out long cylinders called ice cores and read them like a history book. The bubbles show what the air was like in the past.

And here is a surprise. In 2021, scientists studying old soil from under the ice found frozen plant bits, including twigs and moss. Tests published in 2023 showed those plants grew about 400,000 years ago, when parts of Greenland really were green.

{{story:glaciers}}

Around the 4th century BCE, perhaps earlier, a scholar from the north-west of the [[subcontinent|subcontinent]] named Panini wrote the *Ashtadhyayi*, meaning "eight chapters". In about 4,000 short rules, called *sutras*, he described how Sanskrit words and sentences are formed. The rules are so compact they read almost like formulas, with special symbols and abbreviations.

Modern linguists admire Panini as one of the greatest grammarians who ever lived. His [[@grammar-why|grammar]] fixed Sanskrit's form so firmly that later writers, such as the poet [[@kalidasa|Kalidasa]], followed it closely, which is where the "refined" language gets its name. Mathematicians such as [[@aryabhata|Aryabhata]] wrote in Sanskrit verse too, packing numbers and methods into short poems that were easy to memorise.

{{story:vedic-age}}

British Sign Language and ASL are very different, even though both countries speak English. The reason lies in history. In 1817, an American teacher, Thomas Hopkins Gallaudet, brought a deaf French teacher named Laurent Clerc to Hartford, Connecticut. Together they opened the first permanent school for deaf children in the USA.

French Sign Language mixed with the signs that American students already used, and ASL grew from that blend. In 1864, a college for deaf students was founded in Washington, D.C. Today it is Gallaudet University, where classes are taught in ASL and deaf students lead campus life.

{{story:ears-hear}}
